<resources>
    <!--
      PB-DS-2 and PB-DS-3 (S22b): the typography scale.

      18 product text styles across 12 sizes, 4 weights and 5 tracking values. Before this file
      the app expressed zero of them: 1582 lines of surface code held one Typeface.MONOSPACE, one
      Typeface.BOLD, and no setTextSize or setLetterSpacing anywhere.

      EACH STYLE DECLARES THE CSS RULE IT DESCENDS FROM, in the `origin` comment above it. That
      comment is machine-read - android/gate/s22b_type_test.go and the Robolectric suite both
      parse it - and it is the only thing in this file that is a decision. Every NUMBER below is
      read out of docs/research/remote-control-design-directions.html at test time and compared,
      in both directions: a style whose origin is not a text style in the design fails, and a text
      style in the design with no style here fails too. So a size cannot be edited here without
      the design disagreeing, and re-pointing a style at a different rule is a visible claim.

      NO STYLE CARRIES A COLOUR, and that is deliberate. PB-DS-2's sentence lists colour among the
      attributes; the same style renders in several colours - Label.Button is the hero ink on the
      approve variant, the error red on the deny variant and the primary ink on the tertiary one,
      from ONE CSS rule - so a TextAppearance that carried one would be wrong at two of its three
      call sites. Colour is applied by the component kit (PB-DS-6, S23), and the gate asserts the
      absence so it stays a decision rather than becoming a habit.

      SIZES ARE sp, NOT dp. The design is a fixed 386x812 frame and dp would reproduce it exactly,
      which is the argument for dp and it is the wrong one: dp text does not respond to the user's
      font-size setting at all. PB-DS-12 makes surviving a 1.3x scale a floor, and a scale that
      shipped in dp would have decided against it here, silently, for every screen.

      LINE HEIGHT IS AN ABSOLUTE DIMENSION, because Android has no unitless multiplier on a
      TextAppearance. Each value below is the design's multiplier times its own size, and the gate
      recomputes the product rather than trusting it. It appears only on the seven styles whose
      CSS rule declares a line-height; the other eleven inherit the platform's, which is what the
      design does. `.prow .ln b` is the interesting omission: it inherits 1.4 from `.prow .ln` in
      CSS, and on Android it is an inline span inside somebody else's paragraph, where line height
      is the paragraph's property and not the span's. There is nothing for it to carry.

      FAMILIES ARE THE PLATFORM'S (ADR-007 B134 decision 2). The design names SF Pro and SF Mono;
      neither is licensable off Apple, so every style in this app has always rendered a substitute
      chosen by nobody. The decision is `sans-serif` and `monospace` with zero bundled assets.
      Weight 650 is reachable because android:textFontWeight resolves against the platform's
      variable Roboto at API 33, which minSdk guarantees.

      THE RECORDED RESIDUAL IS NOT WHAT THE ADR SAYS IT IS. B134 records that `monospace` may show
      TOFU for U+2500-257F. Measured (MonoBoxDrawingTest), the box-drawing glyphs all resolve -
      through font FALLBACK, at 0.71em against the mono family's own 0.60em. The terminal peek's
      frame therefore renders and is 18 percent wider per character than the text it frames, so it
      does not align. That is a worse failure than tofu, because tofu is visible to anyone who
      looks at the screen once. Bundling JetBrains Mono remains the upgrade path.

      Parents are stated explicitly on every style. `A.B.C` inherits from `A.B` implicitly in
      aapt, and the intermediate names here (TextAppearance.Swarm.Display, .Title, .Label, .Body,
      .Mono) are groupings rather than styles - none of them exists, and an implicit parent that
      does not exist is a build error.
    -->

